    <bioghist>
      <head>Historical Note</head>
      <p>The New England Congregational Church in Manhattan, New York, N.Y., was gathered in 1866.
        At first the church worshipped at a location near the corner of Forty-First Street and
        Sixth-Avenue. For a time, the church worshipped at the Rutgers Chapel while a church
        building was being constructed at the corner of Madison Avenue and Forty-Seventh Street. In
        1870, Rev. Merrill Richardson was installed as the church's minister. In 1872, Rev. Isaac R.
        Prior was ordained and installed as the new minister. The church dissolved in 1872 with its
        membership largely moving to help form the Madison Avenue Congregational Church headed by
        Rev. George Hughes Hepworth. The Madison Avenue Congregational Church building was located
        at Madison Avenue and Forty-Fifth Street.</p>
    </bioghist>
    <scopecontent>
      <head>Scope and Contents</head>
      <p>This collection contains both administrative and membership-related materials for the New
        England Congregational Church in Manhattan, New York, N.Y. The collection includes church
        records and meeting minutes, registers and other membership materials, and a committee
        report on the formation of the church.</p>
    </scopecontent>
    <arrangement>
      <head>Arrangement</head>
